    "lawCode": "Amd §§8-400, 9-209, 10-106, 10-114, 11-202 & 11-212, El L; amd §4, Chap 237 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8376\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:   An act to amend the election law, in relation to mili-\ntary voting, and to amend chapter 237 of the laws of 2005  amending  the\nelection  law  relating  to  military voting, in relation to making such\nprovisions permanent\n\nPURPOSE: To make permanent a current law affecting military voters  that\nwould  otherwise  expire  December  31, 2009, to make it easier for both\nmilitary and other overseas voters to  apply  for  and  obtain  a  paper\nballot for primary, general and special elections and to expand the time\nframe for returning such ballots.\n\nSUMMARY  OF  PROVISIONS:  Section  1  of  the bill amends election law\n8400(2)(d) to provide that military voters and other overseas voters may\nrequest a paper ballot as allowed in subsequent sections  of  the  bill,\ni.e. by facsimile transmission or electronic mail.\n\nSection  2 of the bill amends election law § 9-209(a) to allow boards of\nelections to fix a time for casting and canvassing paper ballots no more\nthan fourteen days after a general or special election and no more  than\neight days after a primary election.\n\nSection  3 of the bill adds a new subdivision 3 to election law § 10-106\nto provide that a military voter may apply  for  a  military  ballot  by\nfacsimile  transmission  pursuant to the Uniformed and Overseas Citizens\nAbsentee Voting Act or by electronic mail.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends election law § 10-114(1) to provide that  a\nmilitary  ballot  will  be  counted  if a) it is postmarked or dated and\nwitnessed no later than the  day  before  the  election  and  b)  it  is\nreceived  by the board of elections no later than seven days following a\nprimary election  or  thirteen  days  following  a  general  or  special\nelection.\n\nSection  5 of the bill adds a new paragraph d to election law § 11202(1)\nto provide that an overseas voter may apply for a special federal ballot\nby facsimile transmission pursuant to the Uniformed and  Overseas  Citi-\nzens Absentee Voting Act or by electronic mail.\n\nSection  6  of  the  bill amends election law § 11-212 to provide that a\nspecial federal ballot cast by an overseas voter will be counted  if  a)\nit  is  postmarked  or  endorsed by a United States government agency no\nlater than the day before the election and b)  it  is  received  by  the\nboard of elections no later than seven days following a primary election\nor thirteen days following a general or special election.\n\nSection  7  of  the  bill amends section 4 of chapter 237 of the laws of\n2005 to repeal the sunset provision and make that  chapter's  provisions\npermanent.\n\nSection 8 of the bill is the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  The  federal  Uniformed  and  Overseas Citizens Absentee\nVoting Act was enacted in 1986 to promote the opportunity  for  military\nand  other  overseas voters to timely apply for and obtain paper ballots\nthat will be cast and  counted.  In  2001,  the  United  States  General\nAccounting  Office  estimated that the act covered 6.1 million citizens,\nincluding 2.7 million active military  personnel  and  their  relatives.\nAmong  military  personnel  who  reported not voting in 2004, 30 percent\nsaid they were not able to vote because their ballots never  arrived  or\narrived too late.\n\nAccording  to the United States Election Assistance Commission, in 2006,\nonly 26.5 percent of the roughly 1 million absentee ballots requested by\nmilitary and overseas Americans were cast and counted.   In early  2009,\nthe  Unites  State Senate Rules and Administration Committee, Chaired by\nSenator Charles Schumer of New York, surveyed election offices in  seven\nstates  having  high numbers of military personnel (California, Florida,\nNorth Carolina, Pennsylvania, Texas, Washington and West Virginia)  with\nthe  assistance  of the Congressional Research Service. The survey found\nthat of the 441,000 absentee ballots requested by eligible voters living\nabroad in 2008, one quarter had not been counted because they were lost,\nreturned as \"undeliverable\" or rejected for various reasons.\n\nThe Pew Center on the States recently issued a report titled No Time  to\nVote:  Challenges  Facing America's Overseas Military Voters. The report\nconcluded that 16 states, or  approximately  one-third  do  not  provide\nenough  time  for  military and overseas voters to complete the absentee\nballot process. The report made suggested changes to  the  election  law\nfor each state that would promote the timely receipt of paper ballots by\nmilitary and overseas voters.\n\nNew  York  State has tried to address this issue since 2005. In order to\npromote voting by military personnel, New York enacted a law that allows\nmilitary voters to sign their ballot envelopes before a  witness  rather\nthan  obtain  a postmark and extended the time frame for receipt of such\nballots by the board of elections to 13 days after a  general  election.\nThis  law  contained a sunset provision and has been reenacted each year\nsince 2005. It is set to expire on December 31, 2009. This  bill  is  an\nattempt to further promote voting by military and other overseas voters.\nTwo  of the Pew Center's suggestions for New York are contained in chap-\nter 237 of the laws of 2005 law and would  be  made  permanent  by  this\nbill.  In  addition, this bill adopts additional Pew Center suggestions,\nextends all of the changes to overseas voters in  addition  to  military\nvoters  and  allows  additional time for receipt of the completed ballot\nafter special elections for the first time.\n\nExtending the time to receive military and other overseas  ballots  will\nbetter  ensure  that no American living overseas will be disenfranchised\nin New York because of the distance and time it takes to send mail  from\nthe far corners of the world.\n\nLEGISLATIVE  HISTORY:  This  makes  permanent chapter 237 of the laws of\n2005 and adds new  provisions  regarding  military  and  other  overseas\nvoters.\n\nFISCAL  IMPLICATIONS: None to the state. The federal government provides\na toll free facsimile number for use by military  and  overseas  federal\nvoters.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
